 (Jul 19,2025) Replied by Tim : sroutk (1) "Domains" are however you set up regions of a chip to operate at different voltages. But yes, there are two transistor types, thin oxide gate and thick oxide gate, which operate at 3.3V and 5.0V, respectively.
(2) I have not looked at their level shifter cells, but yes, normally a level shifter will be part of the high voltage standard cell set because it will need to use the thick oxide devices.
(3) 5.0V and 6.0V are the same device. I have answered a similar question elsewhere, maybe the gf180mcu channel? Anyway, there is only one set of SPICE models and those are labeled as "6V". However, the smallest length devices are only rated up to 5V use. Since the standard cells use the minimum-length devices, the whole standard cell library is rated for 5V. But it still uses the "6V" models. I don't really like the way GF handled that in their PDK, and it wasn't documented well.

 (Jul 19,2025) Replied by Tim :  (Specifically, the pfet_06v0 device is only rated to operate at 5V for lengths less than 0.55um, and the nfet_06v0 device is only rated to operate at 5V for lengths less than 0.7um.) sroutk :  I checked the OSU 12T standard cells, and yes, you are right that the level shifters are incorrect and will fail as designed.

