(In thread) Replied by sroutk : (1) A general question about the level-shifters (LS) in std-cell library: do the synth/P&R tools use the LS ? My understanding was that the synthesis is typically done for one voltage domain. And if the synth uses the LS, how does it take care of the break in the supply rails.  (2) We put the global VDD/GND because the current OSU cells have it that way. And I just checked the GF_5V stdcell lib and it actually has the VDD/GND/VNW/VPW as pins to the cell! So the question now is: Do we also bring the NW/PW connections out as pins ?  And the question is for all cells including the LS 

 (In thread) Replied by Tim : The answers to those questions are a bit involved. . . (1) I expect there are P&R tools that can handle level shifters; it's mostly a matter of whether the tools understand multiple voltage domains. I don't think OpenROAD can handle them. On the other hand, if the cell fits in with the other cells in the library, then it can always be instantiated directly in verilog. The power supply likely would have to be manually routed. 
For the sky130 HVL library, the cell is a double-row cell (which is also a problem for OpenROAD), and the high voltage power supply fits with all the other HVL library cells, and the low-voltage power supply just looks like an over-sized pin. (2) If the OSU cells have global VDD/GND in the schematics, then they should be fixed. That's just bad practice. There are two ways to make standard cells: (A) Put the well and substrate taps inside the cell, or (B) put the well and substrate taps in their own "tap" cell and then make the placement tool add the tap cell at regular intervals throughout the layout so as not to violate latchup DRC rules. For (A), there is only VDD and GND, which is how the OSU standard cells are done. For (B), the well and substrate are not connected to the power rails, so they become extra pins. The purpose of (B) is that it allows the cells to be made a bit smaller. In my experience, this doesn't really gain any significant area savings .
